    "body": "## What this PR does / why we need it\n\n`tests/benchmarks/test_bench_startup.py::test_bench_startup` invokes\n`vllm bench startup` via `subprocess.run(..., capture_output=True)`. This has\ntwo issues that make the test hard to run and debug:\n\n1. **No visible progress.** `capture_output=True` buffers all stdout/stderr of\n   the child process until it finishes. `vllm bench startup` launches 7 LLM\n   instances by default (3 cold + 1 warmup + 3 warm), each involving model\n   loading and `torch.compile`, so it can take tens of minutes. During that\n   time the terminal shows nothing (the tqdm progress bars are swallowed),\n   which looks like a hang. The `print(result.stdout)` / `print(result.stderr)`\n   calls only run *after* the process has already exited, so they don't help.\n\n2. **No timeout.** The call has no `timeout`, so if the subprocess ever blocks\n   (e.g. a stuck worker, GPU memory not released), the test waits forever\n   instead of failing.\n\nThis PR streams the child's output directly to the test's stdout/stderr\n(`stdout=sys.stdout, stderr=sys.stderr`) so progress is visible in real time\n(with `pytest -s`), and adds a generous `timeout=1800` (30 min) so a stuck run\nfails instead of hanging indefinitely. The assertion message no longer embeds\n`result.stderr` (which is now `None` since output is not captured), using the\nexit code instead.\n\nBehavior is otherwise unchanged — the test still asserts `returncode == 0`.\n\n## TestPlan\n\n```bash\n# Run with -s to confirm live progress is streamed and the test passes:\n.venv/bin/python -m pytest tests/benchmarks/test_bench_startup.py -v -s",
